\title{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters for Time Series Foundation Models}

\author{
  Anonymous Author 1 \\
  Anonymous Institution\\
  \texttt{author1@example.com}
  \And
  Anonymous Author 2 \\
  Anonymous Institution\\
  \texttt{author2@example.com}
  \And
  Anonymous Author 3 \\
  Anonymous Institution\\
  \texttt{author3@example.com}
}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Standard mixture-of-experts (MoE) routing on frozen time series foundation models (TSFMs) collapses catastrophically: routing entropy drops to exactly $0.000{\pm}0.000$ in a majority of unfrozen configurations on backbones with Reversible Instance Normalization (RevIN), and remains below $0.55$ in the rest. We diagnose the cause as \textbf{normalization-induced co-adaptation}---RevIN strips per-window mean and scale, while gradient feedback amplifies the collapse. Crucially, standard MoE rescue mechanisms (auxiliary entropy loss, Switch z-loss, load-balance sweeps, ReMoE ReLU routing, expert-choice routing) \emph{cannot} recover the collapse: none restore the temporal statistics the router needs. We formalize this via a mutual-information decomposition (Proposition~1), predicting that datasets with higher location-scale variance benefit more from raw routing---validated at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.96$ ($p{<}0.001$) across seven datasets, including correctly predicting where RR-MoA does not help.
To break the collapse, we propose \textbf{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)}: a lightweight gate routes on the raw pre-normalization input while a frozen backbone produces embeddings for the experts. RR-MoA achieves \textbf{54/54 wins} across 6 datasets $\times$ 3 freeze levels $\times$ 3 seeds ($p{<}0.001$, Wilcoxon), outperforming LoRA, TRACE, independent ensembles, and full backbone fine-tuning. RR-MoA generalizes across four backbones with instance normalization (MOMENT-small/large, Moirai, Moirai-MoE), with Moirai+RR-MoA matching DLinear on Weather ($0.209$ vs $0.208$) and dual-stream experts \emph{beating} DLinear on Weather ($0.198$ vs $0.208$, $p{=}0.03$). As a \emph{negative control}, Chronos (no instance normalization) retains high routing entropy ($1.48$--$1.60$) and RR-MoA provides no advantage---exactly as predicted by the theory, confirming that the collapse is normalization-specific. We uncover a \textbf{Frozen Paradox}: frozen RR-MoA outperforms full fine-tuning of a single-head adapter (all 8 encoder blocks unfrozen) by $12$--$79\%$ across 6 datasets. Within RR-MoA itself, frozen is the best freeze level on 4 of 6 datasets; on Weather and ETTm1, light unfreezing (last-2) wins by up to $13\%$. The paradox is specifically about MoE routing under instance normalization: unfreezing triggers catastrophic routing collapse in hidden-state routers (AdaMix), which RR-MoA escapes by reading the raw signal.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The transformer architecture~\citep{vaswani2017attention} has enabled foundation models~\citep{bommasani2021foundation} across language~\citep{devlin2019bert, brown2020gpt3} and vision~\citep{dosovitskiy2021vit}. Time series foundation models (TSFMs) such as MOMENT~\citep{goswami2024moment}, TimesFM~\citep{das2024timesfm}, Chronos~\citep{ansari2024chronos}, Timer/Timer-XL~\citep{liu2024timer, liu2025timerxl}, Moirai/Moirai-MoE~\citep{woo2024moirai, liu2025moiraimoe}, UniTS~\citep{lee2024units}, Lag-Llama~\citep{rasul2024lagllama}, Time-MoE~\citep{shi2024timemoe}, and TTM~\citep{ekambaram2024ttm} have emerged as powerful pretrained representations for temporal tasks. Once a TSFM is pretrained, a concrete practical question determines whether it is useful in production: \textit{how should the adapter that connects it to a downstream task be designed and trained?}

