\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The transformer architecture~\citep{vaswani2017attention} has enabled foundation models~\citep{bommasani2021foundation} across language~\citep{devlin2019bert, brown2020gpt3} and vision~\citep{dosovitskiy2021vit}. Time series foundation models (TSFMs) such as MOMENT~\citep{goswami2024moment}, TimesFM~\citep{das2024timesfm}, Chronos~\citep{ansari2024chronos}, Timer/Timer-XL~\citep{liu2024timer, liu2025timerxl}, Moirai/Moirai-MoE~\citep{woo2024moirai, liu2025moiraimoe}, UniTS~\citep{lee2024units}, Lag-Llama~\citep{rasul2024lagllama}, Time-MoE~\citep{shi2024timemoe}, and TTM~\citep{ekambaram2024ttm} have emerged as powerful pretrained representations for temporal tasks. Once a TSFM is pretrained, a concrete practical question determines whether it is useful in production: \textit{how should the adapter that connects it to a downstream task be designed and trained?}

\textbf{The one-size-fits-all adapter status quo.} Current TSFM adaptation almost always reduces to the same computational template: \emph{pool the backbone's hidden states $\to$ project to the output horizon}, with a handful of standard variants---linear probing~\citep{goswami2024moment, das2024timesfm}, MLP heads~\citep{nie2023patchtst, wu2023timesnet}, LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora}, and bottleneck adapters~\citep{houlsby2019adapters}. Crucially, every public TSFM benchmark we are aware of uses a \emph{single static} adapter for an entire dataset. Yet real time series are highly non-stationary: a dataset such as ETTh1 mixes quiet baseline segments with sharp seasonal excursions, and it is far from obvious that a single adapter topology is optimal for every window. This observation motivates a \emph{mixture of adapters}: maintain a small pool of topologically distinct expert heads and let a learned router assign each window to the most appropriate expert per sample.

\textbf{Why frozen at all?} The frozen-backbone paradigm is not a limitation: it is the only viable adaptation path in three deployment regimes that a per-dataset DLinear cannot serve. \textbf{(i)~Multi-tenant cloud serving with a shared backbone}: one TSFM sits in GPU VRAM while lightweight per-tenant adapters ($\sim$$1.7$\,MB each) hot-swap from host RAM in microseconds; at $N{=}100$ tenants we measure $141$ tenants/sec swapping throughput and a $45\times$ memory reduction versus naively replicating the full $35$M-parameter backbone per tenant --- a comparison to the worst-case TSFM deployment, not to DLinear (Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}). On raw per-tenant memory alone, $100\times$DLinear ($\sim$$20$\,MB) remains strictly smaller than $1\times$TSFM $+\,$$100\times$RR-MoA ($\sim$$310$\,MB); DLinear's disadvantage is not memory but (a) $O(N)$ training pipelines over potentially private per-tenant data, (b) no zero-shot onboarding, and (c) no cross-task reuse --- all of which are dealbreakers for the remaining two deployment regimes. \textbf{(ii)~On-device and edge deployment}: a single shipped backbone image receives per-site adapter updates over low-bandwidth links, eliminating centralized data collection. \textbf{(iii)~Cross-task reuse}: the same frozen backbone drives forecasting, imputation, and classification via three interchangeable expert pools --- demonstrated empirically across all three tasks in this paper (\S\ref{sec:main_results} and Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}). Lightweight supervised baselines such as DLinear~\citep{zeng2023dlinear} achieve lower absolute MSE on fully-specified individual datasets, but cannot share a backbone across tasks or tenants. Our contribution is not to replace single-dataset specialists, but to optimize routing within the frozen-backbone paradigm where they do not apply. As TSFMs scale toward billion-parameter regimes~\citep{shi2024timemoe}, frozen-backbone PEFT becomes the only viable per-dataset adaptation path.

\textbf{The natural idea that fails: MoE on TSFMs.} When we apply the standard MoE recipe~\citep{wang2022adamix, fedus2022switch}---routing on hidden states with load balancing---to a TSFM with internal RevIN, it \emph{collapses catastrophically}. Across 3 seeds, 3 datasets, and 5 of the 6 unfrozen settings in Table~\ref{tab:adamix}, routing entropy drops to exactly $0.000\pm0.000$ --- all weight concentrates on a single expert. The lone exception (ETTm1 last-2 at $0.218\pm0.309$) and the extended grid (Table~\ref{tab:adamix_extended}) show the same pattern at lower intensity: 7 of 12 unfrozen cells reach exactly $0.000$, the remaining 5 stay below $0.55$ (vs.\ the maximum $\log K = 1.609$).

\textbf{Diagnosis: normalization-induced co-adaptation.} The collapse has a specific causal structure: whichever expert the router marginally prefers receives larger gradients; those flow back into the unfrozen backbone, which reshapes $\mathbf{H}$ to serve that expert better, completing a positive feedback loop within ${\sim}50$ steps (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}). Freezing $\theta$ breaks this loop (Table~\ref{tab:adamix}: entropy recovers to $0.5$--$0.6$), but hidden-state routing still underperforms because the backbone's internal RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin} strips the temporal statistics (trend, amplitude, volatility) that would distinguish samples. A controlled ablation (Table~\ref{tab:router_input}) confirms this: applying RevIN to the router's input degrades MSE by $60$--$88\%$. A cross-backbone control on Moirai~\citep{woo2024moirai} (which lacks RevIN) shows that AdaMix routing \emph{does not collapse} even with unfrozen layers (entropy $1.48$--$1.60$; \S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}), confirming that the failure is normalization-specific, not inherent to MoE on temporal data.

\textbf{Our fix.} We propose \textbf{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)}: (i)~the router reads the raw, pre-normalization input $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$ (preserving per-window trend, amplitude, volatility), and (ii)~the backbone is kept strictly frozen ($\texttt{requires\_grad}{=}\texttt{False}$ for all $\theta$). Only the router and experts are trainable, reducing the optimization from $\theta_\text{active}\cup\phi\cup\psi$ to $\phi\cup\psi$ alone.

\textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} In our 3\,datasets\,$\times$\,3\,freeze levels\,$\times$\,3\,seeds ablation (Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa}), strictly frozen RR-MoA achieves $43.5\%$ and $51.1\%$ MSE reductions over the best fixed adapter on ETTh1 and ETTm1. On these datasets, unfreezing offers no expressivity gain because the normalization-induced co-adaptation loop (which would trigger routing collapse in a hidden-state router) destabilizes the backbone without a compensating benefit for a raw-signal router. Note the paradox is bounded: on Weather and ETTm1 last-2, light unfreezing actually wins by up to $13\%$ within RR-MoA, so the statement is not ``frozen is always best'' but rather ``frozen RR-MoA already extracts most of the available signal without the instability cost of updating the backbone.''

\textbf{Expert pool.} RR-MoA uses five canonical adapter heads (mean-pool, last-token, max-pool, attention-pool, Conv1d-pool). An LLM-guided architecture search (Appendix~\ref{app:aas_details}) confirms that richer code-level search spaces contain strictly better single adapters ($-9\%$ MSE; Appendix~\ref{app:aas_results}), motivating expert diversity.

Our contributions:

\textbf{(1)~An information-theoretic principle for MoE routing on normalized backbones.} We prove a mutual-information decomposition (Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) showing that instance normalization destroys routing mutual information equal to the information in the stripped location-scale statistics. This yields a quantitative prediction: datasets with higher location-scale variance should show larger raw-routing gains---validated at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.96$ ($p{<}0.001$) across seven datasets, including correctly predicting the boundary case where raw routing does \emph{not} help (Traffic; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}). The principle is modality-general: a vision control (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}) exhibits the same gradient co-adaptation mechanism but does \emph{not} collapse under BatchNorm/InstanceNorm, because the routing-discriminative information in images lives in spatial patterns rather than per-channel statistics---exactly as the theory predicts.

\textbf{(2)~Empirical confirmation via five converging controls.} We establish causality with five independent experiments: (a)~disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers routing entropy from $0.000$ to $0.66$--$1.32$; (b)~Moirai (LayerNorm only, no RevIN) does not collapse; (c)~applying RevIN to the router's input degrades MSE by $60$--$88\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:router_input}); (d)~Chronos (no instance normalization, encoder-decoder) is a negative control where RR-MoA provides no advantage---exactly as the theory predicts (\S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}); (e)~a sweep of \textbf{eleven} standard MoE rescue mechanisms (Switch load-balance $\alpha{\in}\{0.1, 1.0, 10\}$, ST-MoE router z-loss, auxiliary entropy regularization at three scales, ReMoE ReLU routing~\citep{zhang2025remoe}, and Zhou et al.\ expert-choice routing~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice}) \emph{all fail} to rescue hidden-state routing on MOMENT$\,+\,$RevIN under unfreezing (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}): mechanisms strong enough to prevent collapse degrade MSE \emph{below} the collapsed baseline, confirming that the normalization bottleneck is a structural information loss rather than an optimization artifact.

\textbf{(3)~RR-MoA and the Frozen Paradox.} We propose \textbf{RR-MoA}, the minimal actionable fix: route the gate on the raw pre-normalization signal while keeping the backbone strictly frozen. RR-MoA achieves \textbf{54/54 wins} across 6 datasets $\times$ 3 freeze levels $\times$ 3 seeds, significantly outperforming LoRA, TRACE-style adapters, independent ensembles, and full backbone fine-tuning ($p{<}0.001$, Wilcoxon; Table~\ref{tab:baselines}). Frozen RR-MoA outperforms full fine-tuning of a single-head adapter by $12$--$79\%$ --- the \textbf{Frozen Paradox} --- a comparison that confounds architecture (MoE vs single-head) with freeze state, so the reversal of the standard PEFT heuristic should be read as ``freezing a 5-expert MoE is better than unfreezing a single-head adapter,'' not ``freezing is always better than unfreezing.'' Within RR-MoA itself, frozen is the best freeze level on 4 of 6 datasets; on Weather and ETTm1 last-2 a lightly unfrozen RR-MoA wins by up to $13\%$, which we attribute to ordinary generalization-gap tradeoffs rather than routing collapse (RR-MoA's raw-signal router is immune to the co-adaptation mechanism by design). RR-MoA generalizes across four backbones with instance normalization (MOMENT-small/large, Moirai, Moirai-MoE). Dual-stream experts that provide adapters with both hidden states and raw input close the DLinear gap from $+57\%$ to $+5\%$ on average and \textbf{beat} DLinear on Weather ($0.198$ vs $0.208$, $p{=}0.03$; \S\ref{sec:main_results}).

\section{Related Work}

\textbf{TSFM Adaptation.} Standard adaptation uses LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} or bottleneck adapters~\citep{houlsby2019adapters} with manually chosen configurations. MSFT~\citep{qiao2025msft} proposes multi-scale finetuning for encoder-based TSFMs. TRACE~\citep{li2025trace} introduces importance-based LoRA selection with reconstructed heads---the closest work, but heads are hand-designed. Beyond LoRA~\citep{gupta2024beyondlora} surveys PEFT alternatives for Chronos. AdaPTS~\citep{benechehab2025adapts} adapts input representations. Prune-then-Finetune~\citep{zhao2025prune} applies structured pruning. In-context fine-tuning~\citep{faw2025icf} adapts at inference time. TEMPLATE~\citep{zhang2025template} addresses model selection; GIFT-Eval~\citep{woo2024gifteval} provides benchmarking. All search over finetuning \textit{strategies}, not adapter \textit{architecture}---the gap we address.

\textbf{MoE in Foundation Models.} Mixture-of-experts routing~\citep{fedus2022switch} has been applied within TSFM backbones: Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} routes frequency bands across internal experts, and Time-MoE~\citep{shi2024timemoe} scales to billions of parameters via sparse activation. These apply MoE \emph{within} the backbone architecture; segment-wise approaches further specialize experts by temporal segments. In contrast, RR-MoA applies MoE at the \emph{adapter level} on a frozen backbone---an orthogonal and complementary design axis, which we empirically validate by evaluating RR-MoA \emph{on} Moirai-MoE (\S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}, Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}): RR-MoA still provides a $-37\%$ MSE improvement on a backbone that already contains internal expert routing, confirming the two design axes stack.

\textbf{MoE routing collapse: we establish a lower bound that is not matched by existing rescue mechanisms.} A large body of work addresses routing collapse and load-imbalance via \emph{optimization-side} interventions: Switch Transformer~\citep{fedus2022switch} adds a differentiable load-balance auxiliary $L_B = \alpha N \sum_i f_i P_i$ with $\alpha{\approx}10^{-2}$; ST-MoE~\citep{zoph2022stmoe} adds a router z-loss $L_z = (1/B)\sum (\log\sum_j e^{x_j})^2$ to penalize large logit magnitudes; Expert-Choice routing~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice} inverts the token$\to$expert picking to expert$\to$token and is auxiliary-loss-free by design; ReMoE~\citep{zhang2025remoe} replaces softmax$+$topK with a ReLU gate and adaptive $L_1$ sparsity. AdaMix~\citep{wang2022adamix} introduces adapter mixtures for NLP but routes on hidden states. \textbf{All of these are symptoms-oriented}: they enforce diversity via optimization constraints rather than addressing the question of whether the router's input contains routing-discriminative information in the first place. In \S\ref{sec:main_results} and Table~\ref{tab:rescue} we run a controlled sweep of \emph{eleven} such mechanisms on MOMENT$+$RevIN and show that \textbf{none of them rescue the collapse}---mechanisms strong enough to prevent entropy from reaching zero do so by actively degrading MSE below the collapsed baseline. Our contribution is complementary to these works: we identify when the \emph{information} is structurally absent from hidden states, in which case no optimization-side trick can help, and we propose moving the router's input out of the normalized representation as the minimal causal fix. To our knowledge, no prior work has identified normalization-induced routing collapse or proposed pre-normalization routing as its remedy.

\textbf{Normalization in Time Series.} RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin} applies reversible instance normalization to mitigate distribution shift. DishTS~\citep{fan2023dishts} learns distribution-specific coefficients to partially recover stripped statistics, and Non-stationary Transformers~\citep{liu2022nonstationary} re-inject series-level statistics into the attention mechanism---both recognizing that instance normalization discards predictive information. We identify a previously unknown consequence of this information loss: RevIN destroys the per-window statistics that MoE routers require for discriminative gating, creating a fundamental tension between normalization for forecasting and normalization for routing. RR-MoA can be seen as operationalizing the ``statistic re-injection'' insight specifically for routing decisions.

\textbf{NAS and LLM-Guided Search.} NAS~\citep{zoph2017nas} has evolved through ENAS~\citep{pham2018enas}, DARTS~\citep{liu2019darts}, and regularized evolution~\citep{real2019regularized}. Shears~\citep{munoz2024shears} and RZ-NAS~\citep{ji2025rznas} search adapter hyperparameters for LLMs; NAS-LoRA~\citep{chen2025naslora} targets vision adapters. FunSearch~\citep{romera2024funsearch}, EvoPrompting~\citep{chen2023evoprompting}, PartEvo~\citep{hu2025partevo}, and ReEvo~\citep{ye2024reevo} demonstrate LLM-guided program synthesis. We use this paradigm for expert pool construction (Appendix~\ref{app:aas_details}).

