\textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} The standard PEFT heuristic holds that unfreezing more parameters monotonically improves downstream performance at some compute cost. We find that this heuristic is \emph{architecturally confounded}: for a 5-expert MoE adapter on a RevIN-normalized backbone, it can reverse. Specifically, two partial statements hold:

\emph{Statement 1 (within RR-MoA).} Across all 6 datasets (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_rrmoa}), strictly frozen is the best configuration on 4 datasets (ETTh1, ETTm2, ETTh2, Electricity). On the remaining 2 (Weather and ETTm1 last-2), light unfreezing wins within RR-MoA by $3$--$13\%$. This variation is ordinary generalization-gap behavior: RR-MoA's raw-signal router is structurally immune to the gradient co-adaptation mechanism (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}) that causes AdaMix to collapse, so the underperformance of unfrozen RR-MoA on the other 4 datasets is not routing collapse --- it is the backbone accumulating distributional drift without a routing-side error signal to absorb it. We explicitly do \emph{not} claim ``frozen is always best'' within RR-MoA.

\emph{Statement 2 (across architectures).} Frozen RR-MoA beats \emph{full backbone fine-tuning of a single-head adapter} (all 8 blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 learning rates) by $12$--$79\%$ on every dataset (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}). This comparison confounds two axes --- freeze state AND architecture (MoE vs single-head) --- so it does \emph{not} directly falsify the single-head PEFT heuristic, which still holds (Best fixed frozen $1.254 \to$ Full-FT unfrozen $1.060$ on ETTh1 is a $15\%$ improvement from unfreezing, exactly as PEFT theory predicts). What Statement 2 does show is that the MoE-vs-single-head axis dominates the freeze-vs-unfreeze axis in our setting, and that investing compute in a frozen MoE is more productive than investing the same compute in unfreezing a single head. This is not an optimization confound for the single-head side: extending full-FT to 50 epochs with cosine scheduling, warmup, and layer-wise LR decay closes the gap by only ${\sim}4\%$ (ETTh1: $1.060 \to 1.017$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}).

\textbf{Causal proof of the hidden-state routing mechanism}: disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers full-FT performance by $54$--$61\%$ (ETTh1: $1.063 \to 0.490$), confirming that RevIN-induced signal loss, not optimization budget, is the mechanism behind the MoE collapse that powers Statement 2.

\begin{proposition}[Gradient Co-Adaptation Drives Entropy Collapse]
\label{prop:frozen}
Consider a $2$-expert MoE with backbone $\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$, router $p{=}\sigma(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{h})$, loss $\ell{=}p\,\ell_1{+}(1{-}p)\,\ell_2$. Under gradient descent on $(A,\mathbf{w})$: \textup{(i)} the dominant expert's loss decreases faster ($\dot{\ell}_k \propto {-}p_k\|\nabla_\mathbf{h}\ell_k\|^2$), creating self-reinforcing collapse toward $p{\in}\{0,1\}$; \textup{(ii)} freezing $A$ breaks this loop (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}).
\end{proposition}

\textit{Remark.} Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} is formalized for a linear backbone ($\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$), but the mechanism extends to deep non-linear networks: locally, a differentiable backbone acts as its Jacobian ($\mathbf{h} \approx J_\theta \mathbf{x}$) in the tangent space of the data manifold, and the same positive-feedback loop drives $\nabla_\theta$ to reshape $\mathbf{h}$ toward the dominant expert. Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}b provides direct empirical confirmation: the per-expert gradient norms in a full 8-block Transformer follow the divergence pattern predicted by the linear analysis, with the dominant expert's gradient growing monotonically while others decay within ${\sim}50$ steps.

\textbf{Routing vs.\ ensembling.} Replacing the learned router with a fixed uniform mixture ($w_k = 1/K$) degrades performance by $30$--$75\%$ on every dataset (ETTh1: $0.899$ vs $0.690$; ETTm1: $1.002$ vs $0.572$; Weather: $0.494$ vs $0.289$). Training the same 5 experts \emph{independently} and averaging predictions (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, ``Ind.\ Ensemble'') is $37$--$46\%$ worse than RR-MoA, confirming that learned per-sample routing---not merely expert diversity---is the source of the gains.

