\textbf{Findings from the 5 complete rescue rows (300 cells).} Three regimes emerge as we sweep entropy regularization strength from $\lambda{=}0.01$ to $\lambda{=}1.0$ and add the ST-MoE z-loss:

\emph{(i) Weak rescue is a no-op.} Entropy reg at $\lambda{=}0.01$ — the most natural ``add a regularizer'' fix — leaves the routing entropy near-collapsed ($0.26$ vs baseline $0.21$) and changes pooled MSE by only $+0.7\%$. The router remains stuck in 73\% of cells.

\emph{(ii) Moderate rescue helps slightly.} Entropy reg at $\lambda{=}0.1$ and ST-MoE z-loss at $c_z{=}0.001$ both partially un-stick the router (entropy rises to $1.03$ and $1.01$ respectively, collapsed-cell fraction drops to $32\%$ and $20\%$) AND marginally improve pooled MSE by $3.3\%$ and $5.6\%$ over baseline. This is opposite to a naive expectation that diversity-enforcing regularizers should hurt MSE.

\emph{(iii) Strong rescue prevents collapse and gives the largest MSE improvement.} Entropy reg at $\lambda{=}1.0$ raises routing entropy to $1.41$ (close to the maximum $\log K = 1.609$), drops collapsed cells to $7/60$ ($12\%$), and improves pooled MSE by $6.5\%$ over baseline. This is the best rescue configuration we have found so far.

\textbf{The key finding survives the rewrite: even the best rescue mechanism cannot close the gap to RR-MoA.} The strongest rescue (entropy reg $\lambda{=}1.0$ at pooled MSE $1.546$) is still approximately $2.3{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA's $0.680$ on ETTh1 alone, and the gap is wider on the harder datasets. The interpretation: optimization-side rescues can \emph{partially compensate} for the routing-information bottleneck — by forcing the router to use multiple experts they recover some of the soft-ensembling benefit — but they cannot close the bottleneck because the missing information (per-window mean and scale, stripped by RevIN) is not in the hidden states at all. Only moving the router's input \emph{out} of the normalized representation, as in RR-MoA, recovers the full signal. This is the precise empirical claim of Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(ii): when the leakage residual $\varepsilon = I(M,\Sigma; S)$ is small, no amount of post-hoc optimization on the router can manufacture information that is not present in $S$.

