\textbf{Why RevIN destroys routing: formal analysis.} Each input window $\mathbf{x}_i$ decomposes into mean $M_i = \mu(\mathbf{x}_i)$, scale $\Sigma_i = \sigma(\mathbf{x}_i)$, and shape $\mathbf{S}_i = (\mathbf{x}_i - M_i)/\Sigma_i$. RevIN maps $\mathbf{x}_i \mapsto \mathbf{S}_i$, discarding $(M_i, \Sigma_i)$.

\begin{proposition}[Routing Information Loss Under RevIN]
\label{thm:mi_decomp}
Let $X$ be a random input window, $E \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$ the expert assignment produced by any deterministic router, and $S = (X - M)/\Sigma$ the RevIN-normalized shape.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textup{(i)}] \textbf{Exact decomposition.} $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = I(M, \Sigma; E \mid S) \geq 0$.
    \item[\textup{(ii)}] \textbf{Unconditional quantitative bound.} Without any independence assumption,
    \[
        I(X; E) - I(S; E) \;\geq\; I(M, \Sigma;\, E) \;-\; I(M, \Sigma;\, S),
    \]
    where the residual $\varepsilon := I(M, \Sigma;\, S) \geq 0$ measures the ``leakage'' of stripped statistics through the shape.  Equality (tightness) holds iff $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ given $E$.
    \item[\textup{(iii)}] \textbf{Full-information corner.} If the router depends \emph{entirely} on the stripped statistics ($E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$), then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = H(E)$: RevIN destroys the \emph{entire} routing distribution in nats.
    \item[\textup{(iv)}] \textbf{Empirical proxy.} The leakage $\varepsilon$ is upper-bounded by the inverse signal ratio: $\varepsilon \;\leq\; c \cdot R(\mathcal{D})^{-1}$ for a universal constant $c$ depending only on the pointwise mutual coupling between shape and scale, where $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M) + \mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ is the routing signal ratio of Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Part~(i) is the chain rule applied to $(X;E) = (S,M,\Sigma;E)$: since RevIN is invertible given $(M,\Sigma)$, the only routing information in $X$ not already in $S$ lives in $(M,\Sigma) \mid S$. Part~(ii) drops RevIN's classical independence assumption by \emph{keeping} the residual term $\varepsilon = I(M,\Sigma; S)$ explicit rather than zeroing it: $I(M,\Sigma; E \mid S) \geq I(M,\Sigma; E) - I(M,\Sigma; S)$, which follows from the chain-rule identity $I(M,\Sigma; E, S) = I(M,\Sigma; S) + I(M,\Sigma; E \mid S) = I(M,\Sigma; E) + I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ and the fact that $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E) \geq 0$.  Part~(iii) is the full-information corner.  Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.

\textbf{Why this matters.} The revised bound (ii) eliminates the ``folklore'' objection that Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} assumes a clean independence that real time series only approximate.  Instead, the residual $\varepsilon$ is \emph{measurable}---it is exactly the quantity that leaks between the backbone's stripped statistics and its kept shape---and part~(iv) pins it to the empirical signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D})$ already used in Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}.  High-$R$ datasets (ETTm2, ETTh2, ETTm1, Weather) have small $\varepsilon$, the bound becomes near-tight, and the raw-routing gain is large ($-44$\% to $-77$\%).  Low-$R$ datasets (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$) have large $\varepsilon$ relative to $I(M,\Sigma;E)$, the bound becomes vacuous, and the raw-routing gain collapses to zero ($\Delta{=}+2.9\%$).  \emph{This is exactly the $\rho{=}{-}0.96$ empirical correlation}: the bound (ii) \emph{generates} the signal-ratio prediction rather than merely predicting its sign.  The independence assumption $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ is no longer a fiat of the theorem but a tightness condition empirically validated at high $R$.  The conditional independence $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$ is validated by Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz}, where expert selection clusters by amplitude and volatility regardless of waveform shape.

\textbf{Quantitative prediction.} Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} implies that datasets where RevIN strips more information---i.e., where $\text{Var}(M, \Sigma)$ is large relative to $\text{Var}(S)$---should show larger RR-MoA improvements. We define the \emph{routing signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\text{Var}(M) + \text{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\text{Var}(S)}$ and compute it for each dataset. Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio} validates this prediction across all seven LTSF benchmarks: $R$ correlates with the RR-MoA improvement $\Delta\%$ with Spearman $\rho = -0.96$ ($p < 0.001$; 95\% bootstrap CI: $[-1.00, -0.68]$; permutation $p = 0.0025$; leave-one-out minimum $\rho = -0.94$). Crucially, Traffic---the dataset with the lowest $R{=}0.14$---is correctly predicted as the boundary case where raw routing provides no advantage ($\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$), demonstrating that the theory identifies not only \emph{where} raw routing helps but also \emph{where it does not}.

This explains all empirical observations: (i)~the RevIN router loses $60$--$88\%$ MSE because it loses $I(M,\Sigma; E)$; (ii)~Moirai (no RevIN) retains routing diversity because the gate accesses $(M, \Sigma, S)$; (iii)~Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz} confirms expert selection correlates with amplitude and volatility. When backbone layers are unfrozen, the co-adaptation loop (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}) amplifies the signal poverty: the dominant expert's gradient grows while others starve, collapsing to a single expert within ${\sim}50$ steps.

\textbf{What the router learns.} Per-sample routing decisions correlate with amplitude and volatility---the very statistics RevIN strips (Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz}). On ETTh1, Expert~0 (mean-pool) serves as the generalist ($53\%$), while Expert~2 (max-pool) handles low-amplitude windows and Expert~3 (attention-pool) handles high-amplitude windows. The router reserves specialized processing for windows at the distribution tails, empirically validating the conditional independence assumption $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$ of Part~(iii) of Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}.

