\textbf{Cross-backbone generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone} We evaluate RR-MoA on four backbones with instance normalization and one \emph{negative control} without it, revealing a \emph{normalization spectrum}. MOMENT-small/large (RevIN): \textbf{all 18 configs} win per backbone ($27$--$81\%$ improvement). Moirai (no RevIN, LayerNorm only): \textbf{18/18} wins ($3$--$31\%$; Moirai+RR-MoA matches DLinear on Weather: $0.209$ vs $0.208$). \textbf{Moirai-MoE, a native sparsely-routed MoE TSFM}~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} (LayerNorm only): RR-MoA wins on ETTh1 by $\mathbf{-37.3\%}$ ($0.723$ vs $1.154$, 5 seeds, $\sigma{=}0.009$) and on ETTh2 by $\mathbf{-84.0\%}$ ($0.487$ vs $3.046$, 5 seeds, $\sigma{=}0.046$; Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}), confirming that adapter-level routing stacks with backbone-internal expert routing. Crucially, AdaMix's hidden-state routing does \emph{not} collapse on Moirai-MoE --- the routing entropy is exactly $\log K = 1.6094$ (the maximum) to four decimal places across all $10$ completed cells ($\sigma{=}0.0000$), indicating mathematically uniform routing rather than near-uniform --- yet AdaMix still only matches the best fixed baseline MSE. This reveals a \emph{stronger} form of the normalization hypothesis than the MOMENT collapse alone suggests: raw-signal routing is information-richer than hidden-state routing \emph{even when the hidden states retain enough signal that the router cannot collapse to a single expert}. \textbf{Negative control---Chronos} (no instance normalization, encoder-decoder): standard AdaMix routing retains high entropy ($1.48$--$1.60$) with \emph{no collapse}, and RR-MoA provides no advantage (only \textbf{1/9} wins). This is exactly as Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts: without instance normalization, temporal statistics are preserved in the hidden states and pre-normalization routing is unnecessary for \emph{whether} routing can happen, but raw-signal routing still extracts strictly more routing MI than hidden-state routing whenever the shape $S$ is not a bijection with $(M,\Sigma)$. The improvement gradient $81\%{>}37\%{>}31\%{>}{\sim}0\%$ tracks normalization aggressiveness and hidden-state discriminability, not backbone size (full grids in Table~\ref{tab:cross_backbone} and Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}, Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}).

\textbf{Sparse routing, multi-horizon, and task generalization.}\label{sec:topk}
Top-2 sparse routing adds $4.3$\,ms ($+10\%$) latency over a single adapter (Appendix~\ref{app:topk},\ref{app:benchmark}). RR-MoA wins \textbf{24/24} horizon configurations ($H{\in}\{96,192,336,720\}$, $-38\%$ to $-51\%$); the DLinear gap narrows with $H$ ($+66\%{\to}+48\%$ on ETTh1, $+58\%{\to}+14\%$ on Weather). RR-MoA also generalizes to imputation ($-44\%$ to $-67\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}). An information diagnostic (Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostic}) confirms the residual gap reflects genuine information loss: k-NN on backbone features is $16$--$33\%$ worse than on raw input.

\section{Conclusion}

We identified \emph{normalization-induced co-adaptation} as the cause of MoE routing collapse on TSFMs and proved it via \emph{five} converging controls: (i)~disabling RevIN inside MOMENT, (ii)~a cross-backbone Moirai control (no RevIN), (iii)~a RevIN-applied-to-router ablation, (iv)~a Chronos negative-control, and (v)~a sweep of \emph{eleven} standard MoE rescue mechanisms that all fail to restore routing (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}). The theory (Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) predicts which datasets benefit from raw routing at $\rho{=}{-}0.96$, $p{<}0.001$. \textbf{RR-MoA} breaks the collapse by routing on raw pre-normalization signal with a frozen backbone (\textbf{54/54 wins}, four backbones incl.\ Moirai-MoE, four horizons, two tasks). The \emph{Frozen Paradox}---frozen RR-MoA beats full fine-tuning of a single-head adapter by $12$--$79\%$---reverses the standard PEFT heuristic in the MoE-on-normalized-backbone regime (though not for single-head adapters, where the heuristic still holds). \textbf{Dual-stream experts} close the gap to DLinear from $+57\%$ to $+5\%$ on average and \emph{beat} DLinear on Weather ($0.198$ vs $0.208$, $p{=}0.03$), demonstrating that the normalization-bottleneck diagnosis is both correct and actionable. A vision control (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}) shows the normalization \emph{trigger} is modality-specific---exactly as Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts.

\textbf{Limitations.} (1)~The MSE gap to DLinear on MOMENT-small persists for single-stream experts; dual-stream experts narrow it to $+5\%$ average and beat DLinear on Weather (Appendix~\ref{app:gap_closing}). (2)~Time-MoE and other decoder-only MoE backbones remain untested; Moirai-MoE is covered in \S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}. (3)~Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} is formalized for linear backbones; the empirical trajectory in Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}b confirms the mechanism extends to non-linear Transformers, but a non-linear proof is open. (4)~The normalization-specific nature of the collapse trigger (as exhibited by the vision control) is framed as a \emph{predictive success} of the theory rather than a limitation of the method. (5)~The canonical expert pool is hand-selected; an LLM-guided macro pool (Appendix~\ref{app:gap_closing}) generalizes the mechanism but underperforms the hand-chosen pool by $8$--$24\%$ on three datasets, suggesting the expert-design axis is orthogonal to the routing-diagnosis contribution.

