\end{tabular}
\end{table}

MOMENT's RevIN applies instance normalization ($\mu{=}0$, $\sigma{=}1$ per window) \emph{before} the encoder, stripping mean and scale from every input. The RevIN inverse is applied after the encoder to restore original units for the loss computation, but the routing decision is made on post-RevIN hidden states. Moirai and Chronos apply only standard LayerNorm within each transformer block, which normalizes across the feature dimension but preserves per-window temporal statistics.

