\end{table}

\textbf{Key finding: normalization prevents rather than causes collapse in vision.} Without normalization (C), the unfrozen backbone freely co-adapts with the dominant expert, collapsing routing entropy to $0.184\pm0.260$ (vs.\ max $1.609$; two of three seeds reach ${\leq}0.075$)---directly confirming the gradient co-adaptation mechanism of Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}. However, adding InstanceNorm2d (A) or BatchNorm2d (D) \emph{prevents} collapse (entropy $1.48\pm0.02$). This is the \emph{opposite} of time series, where normalization enables collapse.

\textbf{Explanation via Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}.} In time series, per-window mean $M$ and scale $\Sigma$ are the primary routing signal ($\rho{=}{-}0.96$); RevIN strips exactly these statistics. In vision, routing-discriminative information resides in \emph{spatial feature patterns}, not in per-channel spatial statistics. InstanceNorm2d strips per-channel spatial mean and variance---which carry negligible routing information for image classification. This is analogous to Traffic (the time-series dataset with lowest routing signal ratio $R{=}0.14$, where RR-MoA provides no advantage). The collapse mechanism (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}) is modality-general; the normalization \emph{trigger} (Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) depends on whether the stripped statistics overlap with the routing signal.

\section{Factorial Decomposition}
\label{app:decomposition}

We decompose the contributions of three factors across 6 evaluation datasets: (1) search space representation (code vs.\ discrete), (2) evolutionary selection pressure, and (3) LLM guidance.

\textbf{Code space $\gg$ discrete space.} All code-space methods outperform discrete evolutionary search (Evo-3epoch, 144 distinct adapter graphs) on 6/6 datasets (Wilcoxon signed-rank $p{=}0.031$, Cohen's $d{=}0.82$). The search space representation is the dominant factor.

\textbf{LLM patterns are individually superior.} Within AAS runs, adapters containing LLM-discovered patterns achieve 19.6\% lower MSE than basic architectures ($p{=}0.003$, Mann-Whitney $U$, $n{=}90$, Cohen's $d{=}0.67$). LLM adapters also use 38\% fewer parameters (mean 110K vs.\ 177K).

\textbf{Validity determines aggregate winner.} The LLM wastes 25\% of evaluations on invalid code; templates achieve 100\% validity.

\textbf{Search budget sufficiency.} We tested 14 budget configurations (20--480 evaluations) across ETTh1 and ETTm1; all beat the conv baseline, even at $B{=}20$ (Appendix~\ref{app:budget}). The curve plateaus around $B\!\approx\!60$.

